# Calendar Reasoning: Odd Days and Weekday Puzzles

> Calendar questions look like trivia — "what day was 15 August
> 1947?" — but they run on a small arithmetic engine: count the
> **odd days** between two dates and the weekday falls out. Learn
> the engine once and every date in history becomes a 30-second
> calculation.

## 1. The Foundation: Ordinary and Leap Years

[definition]
An **ordinary year** has 365 days = 52 weeks + **1 odd day**. A
**leap year** has 366 days = 52 weeks + **2 odd days**. Odd days are
simply the leftovers after removing whole weeks — and leftover days
are what move the weekday.
[/definition]

Leap-year rule (Gregorian):

- Divisible by 4 → leap year; **but** divisible by 100 → not;
  **but** divisible by 400 → leap again.

So 2000 and 2400 are leap years; 1700, 1800, 1900, 2100 are not.
In 400 years there are 97 leap years, giving
$400 \times 365 + 97 = 146097$ days $=$ exactly 20871 weeks — **zero
odd days**. That is why the calendar repeats exactly every 400
years, and why a century always starts with the same two candidate
weekdays.

## 2. Odd-Day Bookkeeping

| Span | Odd days |
| --- | --- |
| Ordinary year | 1 |
| Leap year | 2 |
| 100 years | 5 (24 leap + 76 ordinary → 124 → 5) |
| 200 years | 3 |
| 300 years | 1 |
| 400 years | 0 |

Month codes (odd days within months):

| Month | Odd days | Month | Odd days |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| January | 3 | July | 3 |
| February | 0/1 (leap) | August | 3 |
| March | 3 | September | 2 |
| April | 2 | October | 3 |
| May | 3 | November | 2 |
| June | 2 | December | 3 |

[important]
The **day-number key** used in the standard method:
Sunday = 0, Monday = 1, Tuesday = 2, Wednesday = 3, Thursday = 4,
Friday = 5, Saturday = 6. Total odd days are divided by 7; the
remainder maps directly to the weekday. A negative total? Add 7 —
like counting backwards past midnight.
[/important]

## 3. Finding the Weekday of a Given Date

**Worked example.** What day of the week was 15 August 1947?

- Odd days up to 1600: **0**
- 1601–1900 (300 years): **1**
- 1901–1946: 46 years = 11 leap + 35 ordinary
  = $11 \times 2 + 35 \times 1 = 57$ days $\to 57 \bmod 7 = 1$ odd
  day → **1**
- 1 January – 31 July 1947 (not a leap year):
  $3+0+3+2+3+2+3 = 16 \to 2$ odd days
- 1–15 August: 15 days $\to 1$ odd day.

Total: $0 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 1 = 5$ → **Friday**. (History agrees:
the midnight session of 14–15 August 1947 was a Friday.)

**Worked example.** What day was 26 January 1950 (Republic Day)?

- Up to 1947-end: 0 (1600) + 1 (300 yrs) + odd days 1901–47:
  $11 \times 2 + 36 \times 1 = 58 \to 2$ → total 3.
- 1948, 1949 (ordinary): 1 + 1 = 2 → total 5.
- Jan 1–26, 1950: 26 days $\to 5$.
- Grand total $5 + 5 = 10 \to 3$ → **Thursday**.

## 4. Calendar Repetition Questions

"Which year will have the same calendar as 2024?"

Two conditions decide a true repeat: the later year must start on
the **same weekday** and have the **same leap status** (otherwise
February 29 breaks the match).

**Worked example.** Same calendar as 2023 (ordinary year; 1 January
2023 was a Sunday)? Walk forward, adding each passed year's odd
days: 2024 (+2) → 2, 2025 (+1) → 3, 2026 (+1) → 4, 2027 (+1) → 5,
2028 (+2) → 7 ≡ 0. So 1 January 2029 also falls on Sunday — and
2029 is an ordinary year like 2023. Answer: **2029**.

**Worked example.** Same calendar as 2024 (a leap year starting on
Monday)? A weekday match is not enough here — the repeat must also
be a leap year. Leap years starting on Monday recur roughly every
28 years: 2024 → **2052**. (If a century non-leap year intervenes,
the gap can stretch to 40 years — always verify both conditions.)

## 5. Last-Day and Same-Weekday Puzzles

- "It was Sunday on 1 January 2006 — what was 1 January 2010?"
  Add odd days for 2006–2009: $1+1+2+1 = 5$; Sunday + 5 = **Friday**.
- "Which months of an ordinary year start on the same weekday?"
  In an ordinary year: January and October; April and July;
  September and December; February and March (and November);
  also February–March pair with each other. In a leap year:
  January–April–July, February–August, March–November,
  September–December.
- "A day repeater": the 13th of the month falls on Friday more
  often than any other weekday (a quirk of the Gregorian cycle).

## 6. Exam Patterns and Traps

- **Count the year BEFORE, not the target**: odd days from
  1 Jan 1857 to 1 Jan 1860 use the years 1857, 1858, 1859 — never
  1860. Off-by-one here is the single most common calendar error.
- **February in leap years**: when the span includes a leap
  February *before* your target date, add 2 odd days for that year;
  if the span ends before 29 February, the year still counts as it
  falls.
- **Century years**: 1900 is NOT a leap year — papers love it.
- **Reverse questions**: "X was a Thursday; what day is 100 days
  later?" — $100 \bmod 7 = 2$; Thursday + 2 = Saturday.
- **Date arithmetic across months**: 18 June to 9 July =
  $(30-18) + 9 = 21$ days — always count remaining days of the first
  month plus elapsed of the last.

## 7. Summary

Calendar reasoning is modular arithmetic on leftover days: 1 odd
day per ordinary year, 2 per leap year, 5/3/1/0 per block of
hundred years. Chain the odd days, take remainder mod 7, and map
0–6 to Sunday–Saturday. Watch the two classic traps — the
before-year rule and the century exception — and the chapter
becomes the most mechanical, and therefore the most bankable, of
all reasoning topics.
